                <pb facs="1231" n="1087" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg1087"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00R294/683f: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-02T17:00:00-05:00">November 2, 1929—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>359. Reparation 98. Repeat following to Berlin as Department’s 80.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“Your 201, October 26, 11 a.m. Department is instructing Mr. E. C. Wilson,
                        Acting American Observer with the Reparation Commission, to proceed to
                        Berlin to present draft agreement and to discuss it with representative
                        designated by the German Government. In accordance with American debt
                        settlement precedents, proposal contemplates an agreement which will be
                        signed at Washington by the Secretary of the Treasury after it has been
                        submitted to Congress and Congress has authorized signature.</p>
                    <p>Please make appropriate arrangements and extend Mr. Wilson all possible
                        assistance and facilities.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
